# LF in every checkout, whatever core.autocrlf says (the Git for Windows installer
# sets it to true). Under a non-MSYS bash (WSL, a container, macOS or Linux with
# autocrlf on) a CRLF shell script dies: `set -o pipefail\r` is an invalid option,
# `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` is not found, so every commit is blocked. Not only the
# shell scripts: check-staleness.py fingerprints source bytes, and a CRLF copy of
# an unchanged source read as stale. The tree tracks no .bat/.cmd/.ps1; one that
# is added needs its own `text eol=crlf` line here.
* text=auto eol=lf
# Raw inputs are kept byte-for-byte as deposited: a CRLF CSV or fixed-width file
# under data/ must not be rewritten (its checksum and record lengths are evidence).
data/** -text
*.sh text eol=lf
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# Generated artifacts: never hand-merge them.
#
# docs/*.html and guide/*.html are Quarto build outputs of guide/workflow-guide.qmd.
# A merge conflict in them carries no intent — the correct content is whatever
# `quarto render` produces from the merged source. The keep-ours driver resolves
# them silently; scripts/stamp-render.sh then fails loudly if the committed HTML
# does not match the source it claims to come from, so a forgotten re-render
# cannot ship.
#
# Requires `git config merge.keep-ours.driver true` in each clone — see
# scripts/fork-setup.sh.
docs/*.html          merge=keep-ours
guide/*.html         merge=keep-ours
